    <h1>The <em>Metronome</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">Every page on Drum Town carries a metronome — the tempo pill in the header opens it. Tap the pill and the controls appear across the bottom of the screen. Set a tempo, pick a time signature, and press play. This page is about using it well.</p>
  </header>

  <article class="lesson-prose">
    <h2 class="section-label">Quick Start</h2>
    <p>Tap the tempo pill in the header to open the metronome along the bottom of the screen. Type a tempo (or drag the slider), choose beats per bar — 2/4 through 7/8 — and press play. The first beat of each bar is accented so you always know where "1" is.</p>

    <h2 class="section-label">What Tempo Should I Practice At?</h2>
    <ul class="bulleted">
      <li><strong>Learning something new:</strong> slower than feels necessary — ♩ = 50–70. If it feels boring, it's right. (See <a href="/lessons/slow-practice/">Slow Practice</a>.)</li>
      <li><strong>Cleaning something up:</strong> 10 BPM below the tempo where it falls apart, then move up in small steps.</li>
      <li><strong>Building speed:</strong> raise the tempo ~10% only after a passage is 100% clean for two unbroken minutes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="section-label">Three Metronome Games</h2>
    <p>A click doesn't have to mark every beat. Three ways to make it harder and more useful, from <a href="/lessons/metronome-practice/">Practicing with a Metronome</a>:</p>
    <ul class="bulleted">
      <li><strong>Click on 2 and 4</strong> — halve the tempo and hear the click as the backbeat. The click becomes a bandmate instead of a ruler.</li>
      <li><strong>Click once per bar</strong> — you hold the time for three beats alone and the click grades you on beat 1.</li>
      <li><strong>Subdivision check</strong> — play 8ths or 16ths against a quarter click and listen for the notes between clicks drifting.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The metronome follows you to every lesson on the site, so any exercise you open is one click away from being practiced properly.</p>
  </article>
